---
import FutureTechMissionFrame from '../../../../components/FutureTechMissionFrame.astro';
---
<FutureTechMissionFrame
  missionNumber="7"
  title="Choose Your Path"
  tagline="You know the tools. Now decide what you want to do with them."
  question="What can you create when you get to choose the tools and make the decisions?"
  success="I can choose a pathway, plan a realistic creation, build and test it, improve something based on evidence, and explain the decisions I made."
  nextTitle="Mission 8 — Creator Certification"
  nextHref="../mission-8-creator-certification/"
  try4Text="Check → Trace → Change One Thing → Test Again"
  stages={['Set Up','Plan It','Prototype It','Build It','Level It Up','Checkpoint']}
>
  <div class="creator-flow" aria-label="Mission 7 creator process">
    <span>CHOOSE</span><b>→</b><span>PLAN</span><b>→</b><span>PROTOTYPE</span><b>→</b><span>BUILD</span><b>→</b><span>TEST</span><b>→</b><span>IMPROVE</span><b>→</b><span>EXPLAIN</span>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note"><b>This time, you choose what you create.</b> Keep the idea small enough to finish well.</p>

  <section id="stage-1" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">01</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">CHOOSE YOUR ROUTE</p><h2 class="stage-title">Set Up</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">You have used coding, robotics and 3D design already. This time, <b>you choose one pathway</b> and use your Level 1 skills to make something of your own.</p>
    <p class="concept">Choose the path that fits what you want to create—not the one that looks easiest or has the most parts.</p>

    <div class="path-grid" aria-label="Mission 7 pathway choices">
      <article class="path-card coding">
        <span class="path-code">PATH A</span><strong>CODING</strong><small>SOFTWARE CREATOR</small>
        <p>Build a program, game or digital experience that a user can control.</p>
        <div class="path-tool"><b>TOOLS</b><span>MakeCode for micro:bit or MakeCode Arcade</span></div>
        <p class="choose-if"><b>CHOOSE THIS PATH IF...</b> you want your main creation to happen through code.</p>
        <a href="#stage-2">CHOOSE CODING →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="path-card robotics">
        <span class="path-code">PATH B</span><strong>ROBOTICS</strong><small>SYSTEMS BUILDER</small>
        <p>Build a physical system that senses, decides or reacts.</p>
        <div class="path-tool"><b>TOOLS</b><span>Hummingbird Bit + approved Level 1 hardware</span></div>
        <p class="choose-if"><b>CHOOSE THIS PATH IF...</b> you want code to control something in the real world.</p>
        <a href="#stage-2">CHOOSE ROBOTICS →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="path-card design">
        <span class="path-code">PATH C</span><strong>3D DESIGN</strong><small>PRODUCT DESIGNER</small>
        <p>Design a useful or purposeful object that could be manufactured on a 3D printer.</p>
        <div class="path-tool"><b>TOOLS</b><span>Tinkercad 3D Design</span></div>
        <p class="choose-if"><b>CHOOSE THIS PATH IF...</b> you want to solve a problem by designing a physical object.</p>
        <a href="#stage-2">CHOOSE 3D DESIGN →</a>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="success"><b>Choose one pathway for Mission 7.</b> You may get ideas and troubleshooting help from other students, but your Creator Challenge is your own.</p>
    <p class="mini-note">Not sure? Pick the pathway where you already have one idea you would actually like to try.</p>

    <div class="tool-launch-grid">
      <article><strong>CODING</strong><a class="external tool-launch" href="https://makecode.microbit.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open MakeCode for micro:bit ↗</a><a class="external tool-launch secondary" href="https://arcade.makecode.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open MakeCode Arcade ↗</a><small>Use the platform that fits your project.</small></article>
      <article><strong>ROBOTICS</strong><a class="external tool-launch" href="https://makecode.microbit.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open MakeCode for micro:bit ↗</a><small>Collect your assigned Hummingbird station only after your project receives GO approval.</small></article>
      <article><strong>3D DESIGN</strong><a class="external tool-launch" href="https://www.tinkercad.com/3d-design/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open Tinkercad 3D Design ↗</a><small>You do not need printer access to complete Mission 7.</small></article>
    </div>

    <div class="scope-card"><strong>KEEP IT SMALL ENOUGH TO FINISH</strong><p>Mission 7 is not about building the biggest project. <b>One idea that works well beats five unfinished features.</b></p><div><span><b>CODING</b>One main program, game or experience.</span><span><b>ROBOTICS</b>One controller with a small number of approved inputs and outputs.</span><span><b>3D DESIGN</b>One realistically printable object.</span></div></div>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-2" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">02</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">BEFORE YOU BUILD</p><h2 class="stage-title">Plan It</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Creators do not need to plan every detail before they start—but they do need to know what they are trying to make.</p>
    <p class="concept">Your Creator Plan should take about <b>10–15 minutes</b>. Keep it short. You will learn more once you start building.</p>
    <p class="mini-note">Write these six short answers in the class spot your teacher gives you for Mission 7.</p>

    <div class="plan-grid">
      <article><span>1</span><strong>MY PATH</strong><p>Which path are you taking?</p><small>○ Coding &nbsp; ○ Robotics &nbsp; ○ 3D Design</small></article>
      <article><span>2</span><strong>MY CREATION</strong><p>What do you want to create?</p><small>Explain the main idea in one or two sentences. “I want to create...”</small></article>
      <article><span>3</span><strong>WHAT IT WILL DO</strong><p>What should your creation actually do?</p><small>“It should...”</small></article>
      <article><span>4</span><strong>LEVEL 1 SKILLS</strong><p>Which 2–4 skills will you use?</p><small>Choose the skills that actually matter to your project. Do not select everything.</small></article>
      <article><span>5</span><strong>TOOLS & MATERIALS</strong><p>What do you actually need?</p><small>If your plan depends on equipment we do not have, change the plan before you build.</small></article>
      <article><span>6</span><strong>SUCCESS + CHALLENGE</strong><p><b>I will know it works when...</b><br/>Write one thing you can actually test.</p><p><b>One thing that might be tricky...</b><br/>Predict one part you may need to troubleshoot.</p></article>
    </div>

    <details class="skill-bank">
      <summary>Need help choosing your Level 1 skills?</summary>
      <div class="skill-columns">
        <article><strong>CODING</strong><span>input/event</span><span>variable</span><span>IF / ELSE</span><span>loop</span><span>randomness</span><span>score</span><span>testing/debugging</span></article>
        <article><strong>ROBOTICS</strong><span>Hummingbird ports</span><span>LED</span><span>position servo</span><span>light sensor</span><span>threshold</span><span>IF / ELSE</span><span>Sense → Think → Act</span></article>
        <article><strong>3D DESIGN</strong><span>exact dimensions</span><span>Align</span><span>Group</span><span>Solid + Solid</span><span>Solid + Hole</span><span>Workplane</span><span>printability check</span></article>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="go-gate">
      <span>STOP</span><div><strong>GET A GO CHECK</strong><p>Before you build the full project, show Mr. John your plan.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="go-questions"><span><b>1</b> What are you making?</span><span><b>2</b> What does it need to do?</span><span><b>3</b> Which Level 1 skills will you prove?</span></div>

    <div class="approval-grid">
      <article class="go"><strong>● GO</strong><p>Your project fits Mission 7.</p><small><b>NEXT:</b> Start Prototype It.</small></article>
      <article class="shrink"><strong>◐ SHRINK IT</strong><p>Good idea. The project is too large for this mission.</p><small><b>NEXT:</b> Remove one or more features and show the smaller version. Smaller does not mean worse.</small></article>
      <article class="replan"><strong>○ REPLAN</strong><p>Something important needs to change before you build.</p><small>Possible reasons: missing Level 1 skill · unavailable equipment · unsafe setup · too easy · far beyond Level 1.</small></article>
    </div>

    <div class="waiting-card"><strong>WAITING FOR A GO CHECK?</strong><p>Do not stand beside Mr. John.</p><div><span>1. Open your software.</span><span>2. Review the Mission 1–6 skill you plan to use.</span><span>3. Organize only the equipment you are allowed to collect.</span><span>4. Sketch or think through the first working version.</span></div><small><b>Robotics:</b> do not take limited Hummingbird equipment until your project receives GO unless Mr. John tells you otherwise.</small></div>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-3" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">03</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">MAKE THE IMPORTANT PART WORK FIRST</p><h2 class="stage-title">Prototype It</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Do <b>not</b> build the whole idea yet. Build the smallest version that proves your main idea can work.</p>
    <p class="concept"><b>CORE FIRST:</b> Make it work before you make it bigger, prettier or more complicated.</p>

    <p class="path-instruction"><b>Open only your path.</b> The other routes are here for other creators.</p>
    <div class="path-details">
      <details id="coding-prototype" class="path-detail coding">
        <summary><span>PATH A</span><strong>CODING — YOUR FIRST TARGET</strong></summary>
        <p>Make the main interaction work. Ignore extra screens, decorations and bonus features for now.</p>
        <div class="route"><span>INPUT</span><span>CODE</span><span>DECISION</span><span>RESULT</span></div>
        <p><b>Can the user do something and get the response you intended?</b></p>
        <div class="check-list"><span>☐ My project starts or receives an input.</span><span>☐ My important code runs.</span><span>☐ I get a visible result.</span><span>☐ I can test it again.</span></div>
        <p class="mini-note">Example, not a requirement: Button press → change variable → IF decision → display result.</p>
      </details>

      <details id="robotics-prototype" class="path-detail robotics">
        <summary><span>PATH B</span><strong>ROBOTICS — YOUR FIRST TARGET</strong></summary>
        <p>Make one input or trigger control one physical output. Test the technical system before building a fancy body, sign or structure around it.</p>
        <div class="route"><span>SENSE / INPUT</span><span>CODE</span><span>DECIDE</span><span>ACT</span></div>
        <div class="check-list"><span>☐ My hardware uses the correct ports.</span><span>☐ My system powers on safely.</span><span>☐ My code reaches the Hummingbird.</span><span>☐ At least one output does what I expect.</span></div>
        <p class="warning"><b>Unsure about wiring or power? Stop and ask.</b> Safety questions do not count against TRY 4.</p>
      </details>

      <details id="design-prototype" class="path-detail design">
        <summary><span>PATH C</span><strong>3D DESIGN — YOUR FIRST TARGET</strong></summary>
        <p>Build the main form at the correct size. Do not spend your first 30 minutes decorating.</p>
        <div class="route"><span>SHAPE</span><span>SIZE</span><span>POSITION</span><span>GROUP / CUT</span></div>
        <div class="check-list"><span>☐ My main dimensions make sense.</span><span>☐ My important shapes are positioned correctly.</span><span>☐ The core object matches my plan.</span><span>☐ I can explain what each major shape is doing.</span></div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="decision-gate"><strong>DOES THE CORE IDEA WORK?</strong><div><span class="yes"><b>YES</b> Continue to Build It.</span><span class="almost"><b>ALMOST</b> Fix the core problem before adding features.</span><span class="no"><b>NO</b> Use TRY 4. If the original idea is not working, shrink the project.</span></div></div>

    <div class="try4 mission7-try4"><strong>STUCK? TRY 4</strong>
      <div class="try4-grid">
        <article><b>1 — CHECK</b><p>What exactly is not working? Do not stop at “It doesn't work.”</p></article>
        <article><b>2 — TRACE</b><p><strong>Coding:</strong> Input → Code → Decision → Result<br/><strong>Robotics:</strong> Input/Sensor → Port → Code → Output<br/><strong>3D:</strong> Shape → Size → Position → Group/Cut</p></article>
        <article><b>3 — CHANGE ONE THING</b><p>Change one possible cause. Do not change five things at once.</p></article>
        <article><b>4 — TEST AGAIN</b><p>Did the change help?</p></article>
      </div>
      <p>Still stuck? Ask another creator: <b>“What do you notice?”</b> Then ask Mr. John.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="help-script"><strong>READY TO ASK FOR HELP?</strong><span>I expected __________.</span><span>Instead, __________ happened.</span><span>I checked __________.</span><span>I changed __________.</span></div>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-4" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">04</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">COMPLETE THE CREATOR CHALLENGE</p><h2 class="stage-title">Build It</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Your core idea works. Now finish the <b>minimum version</b> of your Creator Challenge.</p>
    <p class="warning"><b>Complete the requirements for your path before adding bonus features.</b></p>

    <p class="path-instruction"><b>Open only your path.</b> The other routes are here for other creators.</p>
    <div class="path-details">
      <details id="coding-build" class="path-detail coding">
        <summary><span>PATH A</span><strong>CODING / SOFTWARE CREATOR</strong></summary>
        <div class="requirement-list"><span>☐ <b>One meaningful input</b><small>A button, controller input or other user action.</small></span><span>☐ <b>One variable or changing value</b></span><span>☐ <b>One IF or IF/ELSE decision</b></span><span>☐ <b>One repeating behaviour or loop</b></span><span>☐ <b>A clear purpose, rule or goal</b></span><span>☐ <b>A working test</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one change after testing</b></span></div>
        <p class="success">You do not need to use every block you learned in Level 1. Use the ones that make sense for your project.</p>
        <div class="idea-bank"><strong>NEED AN IDEA?</strong><span>reaction game</span><span>digital pet</span><span>quiz</span><span>random challenge generator</span><span>score game</span><span>classroom timer</span><span>decision helper</span><span>virtual dice</span><span>simple arcade challenge</span></div>
        <p class="boundary"><b>KEEP IT LEVEL 1:</b> avoid online multiplayer, databases, external APIs, another programming language, many game levels, or a huge amount of new code. <b>If the project needs weeks instead of classes, shrink it.</b></p>
      </details>

      <details id="robotics-build" class="path-detail robotics">
        <summary><span>PATH B</span><strong>ROBOTICS / SYSTEMS BUILDER</strong></summary>
        <div class="requirement-list"><span>☐ <b>At least one physical output</b></span><span>☐ <b>One coded behaviour</b></span><span>☐ <b>One meaningful input, trigger or decision</b></span><span>☐ <b>Correct hardware ports</b></span><span>☐ <b>Reliable repeated operation</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one test</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one change after testing</b></span></div>
        <p class="mini-note"><b>Recommended Level 1 hardware:</b> LED · position servo · Hummingbird light sensor · micro:bit inputs already learned. New hardware requires teacher approval.</p>
        <div class="idea-bank"><strong>NEED AN IDEA?</strong><span>automatic night light</span><span>warning signal</span><span>robotic signal arm</span><span>light-responsive sculpture</span><span>animated cardboard creature</span><span>desk status indicator</span><span>security indicator</span><span>reaction device</span></div>
        <p class="boundary"><b>KEEP THE SYSTEM MANAGEABLE:</b> standard Mission 7 build = <b>1 controller + 1 input/sensor + 1–2 outputs.</b> Avoid large machines, autonomous vehicles, many sensors, complicated mechanical systems, permanent equipment modifications, and soldering.</p>
        <p class="concept"><b>Build the system first. Decorations come later.</b></p>
      </details>

      <details id="design-build" class="path-detail design">
        <summary><span>PATH C</span><strong>3D DESIGN / PRODUCT DESIGNER</strong></summary>
        <div class="requirement-list"><span>☐ <b>A clear purpose</b></span><span>☐ <b>Intentional dimensions</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least three intentionally placed shapes</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one grouped construction</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one purposeful cut, hole, slot or recess</b></span><span>☐ <b>A printability check</b></span><span>☐ <b>At least one revision</b></span></div>
        <div class="idea-bank"><strong>NEED AN IDEA?</strong><span>cable organizer</span><span>locker tag</span><span>game piece</span><span>name plate</span><span>simple stand</span><span>storage marker</span><span>headphone hook</span><span>key tag</span><span>desk organizer component</span><span>small holder</span></div>
        <p class="boundary"><b>DESIGN FOR THE CLASSROOM:</b> avoid very large prints, complex print-in-place models, extensive supports, many separate parts, or downloaded models used as your own design.</p>
        <p class="success"><b>You do not need a physical print to complete Mission 7.</b> Your digital design and explanation are the evidence.</p><p class="mini-note"><b>Print scope:</b> Mission 6’s size limits were for the Maker Tag challenge, not universal 3D-printing rules. Keep your Mission 7 design small and let the GO Check confirm whether it is realistic for our classroom print queue.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="test-panel">
      <p class="mini-label">SHARED CREATOR PROCESS</p><h3>Test It Like a Creator</h3>
      <p>Do not ask, “Does it look finished?” Ask: <b>“Does it actually do what I said it would do?”</b></p>
      <div class="evidence-grid"><article><strong>I TESTED...</strong><p>What did you actually do?</p></article><article><strong>I EXPECTED...</strong><p>What should have happened?</p></article><article><strong>WHAT HAPPENED...</strong><p>What did you observe?</p></article></div>
      <h3>Change Something for a Reason</h3>
      <p>Based on your test, choose one thing to improve.</p>
      <div class="evidence-grid"><article><strong>I CHANGED...</strong></article><article><strong>BECAUSE...</strong></article><article><strong>AFTER THE CHANGE...</strong><p>Explain briefly or demonstrate it.</p></article></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-5" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">05</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">ONLY AFTER THE CORE PROJECT WORKS</p><h2 class="stage-title">Level It Up</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Your project already meets the Mission 7 requirements. Now you may make <b>one meaningful improvement</b>.</p>
    <p class="warning"><b>Do not restart your project.</b> Improve what already works.</p>

    <p class="path-instruction"><b>Open only your path.</b> Choose one meaningful improvement.</p>
    <div class="path-details compact">
      <details class="path-detail coding"><summary><span>PATH A</span><strong>CODING</strong></summary><div class="level-options"><span>second useful control</span><span>clearer feedback</span><span>improved scoring</span><span>adjusted difficulty</span><span>useful animation or sound</span><span>clearer instructions</span><span>better error handling</span></div><p><b>Ask:</b> What will become better for the user?</p></details>
      <details class="path-detail robotics"><summary><span>PATH B</span><strong>ROBOTICS</strong></summary><div class="level-options"><span>second output</span><span>better threshold behaviour</span><span>LED + servo</span><span>clearer response</span><span>better physical mounting</span><span>improved reliability</span><span>improved housing</span></div><p><b>Ask:</b> How will this make the system work better?</p></details>
      <details class="path-detail design"><summary><span>PATH C</span><strong>3D DESIGN</strong></summary><div class="level-options"><span>better stability</span><span>better fit</span><span>better dimensions</span><span>less unnecessary material</span><span>cleaner alignment</span><span>useful personalization</span><span>better ergonomics</span><span>easier printing</span></div><p><b>Ask:</b> What problem does your improvement solve?</p></details>
    </div>
    <p class="success"><b>One good improvement is enough.</b> Do not keep adding features just because you have time.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-6" class="stage checkpoint">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">06</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">SHOW WHAT YOU CAN DO</p><h2 class="stage-title">Checkpoint</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Your checkpoint is short and individual. You are not giving a presentation to the class. You are showing that <b>you understand the creation you built.</b></p>

    <div class="ready-card"><strong>READY-FOR-CHECK</strong><div class="check-list"><span>☐ My minimum project works.</span><span>☐ I completed one real test.</span><span>☐ I made or attempted one improvement.</span><span>☐ My project is saved.</span><span>☐ My equipment/workspace is organized.</span><span>☐ I can explain one important technical decision.</span></div><span class="ready-state">READY? ADD YOUR NAME TO THE CLASS READY-FOR-CHECK SYSTEM.</span></div>

    <div class="waiting-card"><strong>WAITING FOR YOUR CHECK?</strong><p>Keep working quietly.</p><div><span>Test your project again.</span><span>Clean up unnecessary code or shapes.</span><span>Improve instructions.</span><span>Organize hardware.</span><span>Help another creator troubleshoot.</span><span>Complete one Level It Up improvement.</span></div><small><b>Do not dismantle your project until your checkpoint is complete.</b></small></div>

    <div class="checkpoint-steps">
      <article><span>1</span><strong>SHOW IT</strong><p>Mr. John: <b>“Show me what it does.”</b></p><small>Demonstrate the core technical behaviour.</small></article>
      <article><span>2</span><strong>EXPLAIN IT</strong><p>Answer one pathway-specific technical question.</p><small>Be ready to explain one important part of your project.</small></article>
      <article><span>3</span><strong>TELL ME WHAT CHANGED</strong><p>Mr. John: <b>“What did you test or improve?”</b></p><small>Explain what you noticed, changed, and whether it helped.</small></article>
    </div>

    <p class="path-instruction"><b>Open your path.</b> Be ready for one question—not all of them.</p>
    <div class="checkpoint-questions">
      <details class="path-detail coding"><summary><span>PATH A</span><strong>CODING — BE READY TO EXPLAIN ONE</strong></summary><div class="question-list"><span>What starts this action?</span><span>What does this variable keep track of?</span><span>Where does your program make a decision?</span><span>What repeats?</span><span>What happens when this condition is false?</span><span>Why did you choose this input?</span></div></details>
      <details class="path-detail robotics"><summary><span>PATH B</span><strong>ROBOTICS — BE READY TO EXPLAIN ONE</strong></summary><div class="question-list"><span>What does the system sense or receive?</span><span>Which port is this connected to?</span><span>Where does the code make a decision?</span><span>What controls this output?</span><span>What does your threshold do?</span><span>What would happen if this value changed?</span></div></details>
      <details class="path-detail design"><summary><span>PATH C</span><strong>3D DESIGN — BE READY TO EXPLAIN ONE</strong></summary><div class="question-list"><span>Why did you choose these dimensions?</span><span>Which shapes are grouped?</span><span>Where did you use a hole or cut?</span><span>How did alignment help?</span><span>What makes this design printable?</span><span>What did you change after testing or checking the model?</span></div></details>
    </div>

    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <article class="complete"><strong>MISSION COMPLETE</strong><p>You showed the required Level 1 skills and explained your work.</p><small><b>NEXT:</b> Save your final project and Mission 7 evidence.</small></article>
      <article class="retry"><strong>RETRY ONE SKILL</strong><p>Most of the mission is complete. One skill needs another try.</p><small>Fix that skill, then return to Ready-for-Check.</small></article>
      <article class="support"><strong>SUPPORT ROUTE</strong><p>You need more support before this Creator Challenge is complete.</p><small>Work with Mr. John on the next small step.</small></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stage passport">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">✓</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">SKILL PASSPORT</p><h2 class="stage-title">Creator Independence</h2></div></div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>☐ I can plan a project that fits the tools and time available.</span><span>☐ I can choose Level 1 skills that fit what I want to create.</span><span>☐ I can build and test a working version of my idea.</span><span>☐ I can troubleshoot one part of a system at a time.</span><span>☐ I can improve my creation based on testing.</span><span>☐ I can explain an important technical decision I made.</span></div>
    <p class="success"><b>MISSION 7 COMPLETE // CREATOR CHALLENGE READY</b></p>
  </section>

  <section class="stage evidence-summary">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">◎</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">WHAT COUNTS</p><h2 class="stage-title">Your Mission 7 Evidence</h2></div></div>
    <div class="evidence-five"><span><b>PLAN</b>Short approved Creator Plan.</span><span><b>BUILD</b>Working program, robotic system or 3D model.</span><span><b>TEST</b>One clear test.</span><span><b>IMPROVE</b>One change based on evidence.</span><span><b>EXPLAIN</b>Your individual checkpoint.</span></div>
    <p class="concept">You do <b>not</b> need a long project report.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="stage collaboration">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">↔</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">CREATOR COMMUNITY</p><h2 class="stage-title">Build Your Own. Help Each Other.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="collab-grid"><article><strong>YOU MAY</strong><span>suggest an idea</span><span>help locate a block or tool</span><span>look for a bug together</span><span>test another student's project</span><span>explain a technique</span><span>share a hardware station when required</span></article><article><strong>YOU MAY NOT</strong><span>build someone else's project</span><span>copy an entire program</span><span>make someone else's CAD model</span><span>answer their checkpoint for them</span></article></div>
